Z||000000||bHooper||10-10-1999||03:58 PM||||i looked at a 88 formula that i am thinking of buying.  it has the getrag 5 speed and is difficult to put into gear, at start or stop, it also displays vibration in the clutch pedal when fully depressed.  (this also is more prominant if i rev the engine)  anyone have any thoughts on this?   the car has 123000 miles on it (with the original clutch) and is clean as a new car.  i am really thinking of buying this car, has i can get it for less than $1000...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||10-10-1999||04:07 PM||||<BR>Hmmm... I'm not sure what the significance is of vibration in the clutch pedal. It's not as if the clutch pedal is directly connected to the engine/transaxle assembly. The hydraulic line certainly wouldn't transmit any vibration from the clutch/tranny.<P>For less than a thousand bucks, it's probably worth it no matter what the problem.||||reg||14
Z||000002||bHooper||10-10-1999||04:19 PM||||i would normally think that too, except that the reason i'm looking to buy another car is that my 86.5 gt's getrag is scrap metal....2 fiero's for scrap don't do me a whole lot of good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||10-10-1999||07:10 PM||||Sounds like the clutch springs are really messed up,or that and the release bearing.<BR>Could be a sign of abuse,might be a problem if the mileage is high.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||10-10-1999||07:52 PM||||the car is a  one owner and he "said" he never ran the car hard.  normally I would question that, but he didnt seem like the type to do it, besides other than that the car is perfect.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||10-10-1999||08:13 PM||||It's possible that the throw out bearing is going bad, clutch disk is screwed up, or the pressure plate/flywheel is warped. Brakes are hydraulic too, but you can still feel a pulsation in the pedal when a rotor is warped. If you buy it, assume you'll have to do some clutch work. I doubt it is a tranny problem, sounds more like clutch trouble. The owner probably got a shop estimate and freaked out at the expense. If you can work on it yourself, you'll save a bundle. It's your call.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||10-10-1999||11:41 PM||||Oh yeah,Batboy has a point;does it pulse when pushing down,or releasing?<BR>Releasing could indicate a warped flywheel/pressure plate,just pushing down could be bearing/pressure plate(springs).<BR>Sounds like a good deal though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||10-11-1999||06:58 AM||||it pulses of vibrates when I push the pedal in.  so it sounds like I'm going to have to rebuild the tranmission, hmm I don't know what to do then.  that probably will cost at least $400, and I'm bac to square one, when I could buy a tranny for the GT I allready have and rebuild that car and be happy...||||reg||2
Z||000009||theogre||10-11-1999||09:02 AM||||The throw out bearing being shot is about the only common thing that should cause feed back in the clutch peddle. The only other posibility is the thrust bearings on the crank allowing to much runout.<P>The throwout and the pressure plate have no contact with the tranny that would do it. If the clutch was dragging it would be a troll to shift, but rarely could it feed back unless the pressure plate or fly wheel is extremely warped.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||10-11-1999||09:20 AM||||Yes, it does shift "like a troll", and yes there is feedback in the pedal.  My other car had the throwout bearing and pressure plate go in it once, the car shuttered and went for a ride on it's own  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>so it sounds like, either way, an expensive transmission job?  money that i could put into my own car (that doesnt have a tranny) and have the 1 car i want, instead of having one without a tranny and having another car with a tranny in need of serious surgery....correct?||||reg||5
Z||000011||fieroshop||10-11-1999||11:41 AM||||I believe the problem is a bad pressure plate and throwout bearing. When a person depresses the pedal and there is vibrations then you more then likely have a bad pressure plate that is warped. I should know I've even had a special clutch built for my shop because of too many failures in the Fiero. You can check it out at <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A> <BR>Forgot to say that once there you need to click on the services button<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 10-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||10-12-1999||06:17 PM||||It sounds like the clutch disk friction material is coming loose and possibly falling apart.  Mine did the same thing, chattered upon engaging and disengaging.  I could still shift if I behaved myself and didn't run the rpms up.  When I pulled the clutch out I found that one side of the clutch disk had only two pieces of friction material about the size of silver dollars still attached.  Luckily I didn't score the flywheel.<BR>I bought an OEM new clutch disk, pressure plate, throwout bearing, pilot bearing from AutoZone for $105.00.  It is a Borg Warner unit and it looks pretty good.  I am replacing my motor with a Grooms stock long block from Ed Parks.<BR>Changing out a clutch isn't nearly as bad as I thought it would be.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||10-12-1999||06:21 PM||||By the way, the standard transmissions do not require the use of a pilot bearing.  The shaft isn't long enough to engage it.  The automatic transmission does require the use of a pilot bearing since the shaft is longer and does indeed engage the bearing.<BR>I got this info from Ed Parks.  he said they have measured many transmission shafts to come up with this tidbit of info.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick||10-12-1999||07:43 PM||||<BR>Carroll, I'm not arguing with you, but in my relative limited experience over the years dealing with manual transmissions, I can't imagine the tranny input shaft not engaging the pilot bushing. This would seem to be a rather crippled way to assemble the transmission to the engine.||||reg||5
Z||000015||David||10-12-1999||08:24 PM||||Patrick<P>The Fiero doesn't use a pilot bushing in the crankshaft because the input shaft goes the entire length if the transmission.  This is unlike what is considered a normal front engine, rear wheel drive transmission where the input shaft only goes part of the way into the trans and needs the crankshaft bushing for support.  The Fiero input shaft is supported by two tapered roller bearings that are quite a distance apart, giving the shaft all the support needed.  Look at a cross-sectional picture of the Fiero transmission and compare it with what you are familiar with and I think you will see the difference.  <P>David<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Patrick||10-12-1999||08:54 PM||||<BR>Oh... ok, that makes sense. I've only worked with front engine/rear wheel drive combinations before. The input shafts in those trannys are very short with no real support except for the one set of bearings they go through. You can imagine my surprise at reading Carroll's post when I didn't realize that there was such a difference in input shafts. Thanks David.<P>I think we need to hear from Ogre about this. He had me (and probably many others) concerned when he stated that there could sometimes be a problem with the input shaft binding in the pilot bushing in manual tranny equipped Fieros. Could Ogre have been mistaken...? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>OGRE !!!||||reg||14
Z||000017||mwbackus||10-12-1999||09:03 PM||||He was probably referring to the clutch fork shaft, the fork shaft bearing tolerances are totally messed up, and have been know to cause binding problems, even on brand new Fiero getrags.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||10-12-1999||09:26 PM||||Carroll, to bad you aren't in North Carolina, since you don't seem to mind working on Fiero transmissions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  I am still debating about the affordability of the car, spend money on a new one when I could go a long way with the GT that I have...<BR>Rodney Dickman suggest the clutch and pressure plate are the problem, I sent Ed Parks a email, but haven't heard from him yet.  To replace the pressure plate/clutch/throw out bearing at the dealership will be between $850-1100, at the local mecanic its $450 for everything or $350 if I supply my on parts (fiero shop kevlar clutch).  So, I'm back to square one...$1400 buys a Getrag, new brake rotors, and rebuild kit for my existing GT.  What to do? ||||reg||10
Z||000019||lowCG||10-12-1999||09:58 PM||||Sounds like,basically,the car needs a clutch,however,when the work is being done,have the mechanic keep the old parts around to compare with whatever new parts you get(there are a few different pressure plates that will all bolt up,one being correct)and make sure the clutch slave cylinder rod isn't part of the problem,sometimes people substitute engine pushrods or whatever they can get to try and make their clutch work again.Have seen the springs destroyed by this before.<BR>Also,on my car,the pilot bearing was actually in the crank,just not used.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Carrolles||10-13-1999||07:27 AM||||Keep in mind that if you have to have the flywheel surfaced you should have a spacer installed between the flywheel and crank to make up for the lost thickness that was machined off.  There isn't much excess travel in the clutch hydraulics.||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||10-13-1999||09:19 AM||||the manual tranny doesn't need a pilot? Hmmmm. That is diferant... even most FWD cars I've worked on needed it. Haven't had a fero stick apart..... That would pretty much limit the pedal feedbackto either a screwy clutch or throwout then.<P>As for what to do,,, why don't you change the thing yourself. clutch work isn't hard. common sence and taking your time are about all thats required. IF there is enough room to move the tranny clear of the clutch, you could likely take it thing out the top if you support the motor from overhead instead of racking it across the car body.||||reg||1
